<p>
  An <strong>anemometer</strong> is an instrument that measures the speed of moving air — and, in most digital models, air temperature, airflow volume, and wind chill as well. It is a core tool for HVAC commissioning, building ventilation audits, cleanroom validation, and industrial process airflow checks. This guide explains the different sensing technologies, what the readings mean, and how to choose the right type for your work.
</p>

<h2 id="sensor-types">Sensor Types — Vane, Hot-Wire, and Cup</h2>

<p>Digital anemometers use one of three sensing methods, each suited to a different measurement situation.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Vane (impeller) anemometers</strong>: A small rotating fan-like vane spins in the airstream; the rotation speed is converted to an air velocity reading. Vane probes are rugged, inexpensive, and well suited to duct velocity and open-area airflow measurement in the 0.4–30 m/s range typical of HVAC work.</li>
  <li><strong>Hot-wire (thermal) anemometers</strong>: A heated element loses heat to the passing air; the cooling rate is proportional to air velocity. Hot-wire probes are far more sensitive at very low velocities (down to ~0.1–0.2 m/s) and their thin probe tip reaches into small ducts, diffuser slots, and cleanroom grilles where a vane won't fit.</li>
  <li><strong>Cup anemometers</strong>: Three or four cups mounted on a rotating spindle, almost always used for outdoor/meteorological wind speed measurement rather than handheld indoor work, since they respond well to wind direction changes but are too large and slow-responding for duct work.</li>
</ul>

<Callout type="tip" title="Choosing between vane and hot-wire">
  If you're measuring duct velocity, open room airflow, or outdoor wind speed, a vane probe is the practical, durable choice. If you're validating a cleanroom laminar flow hood, a fume hood face velocity, or airflow through a small diffuser slot, a hot-wire probe's sensitivity at low velocity and its slim tip are worth the extra cost.
</Callout>

