<h2 id="duct-measurement">Measuring Duct Airflow Correctly</h2>

<p>
  Air velocity in a duct is never perfectly uniform across the cross-section — it is highest at the centre and drops toward the duct walls due to friction. A single-point reading anywhere in the duct will not give a representative average. The standard method is a <strong>traverse</strong>: dividing the duct cross-section into a grid of equal-area segments (typically 16–25 points for a rectangular duct) and averaging the velocity readings taken at the centre of each segment.
</p>

<p>Multiplying the traverse-averaged velocity by the duct's cross-sectional area gives the volumetric airflow rate — the number that actually matters for balancing an HVAC system or verifying design airflow.</p>

<h2 id="accuracy-response">Accuracy and Response Time</h2>

<p>
  Look for stated accuracy as a percentage of reading plus a fixed offset (e.g. ±3% + 0.2 m/s) rather than a single flat number, since anemometer error typically grows at the low end of the range. Response time matters for fluctuating airflows — a slow-responding sensor will smooth out real gusts and turbulence, giving an artificially stable-looking reading that hides the true variation.
</p>

<h2 id="applications">Common Applications</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>HVAC commissioning and balancing</strong>: Verifying supply/return air volumes match design specification at each diffuser and duct branch.</li>
  <li><strong>Cleanroom and fume hood validation</strong>: Confirming face velocity meets the standard required for the containment or cleanliness class.</li>
  <li><strong>Building ventilation audits</strong>: Checking natural or mechanical ventilation rates against occupancy or industrial hygiene requirements.</li>
  <li><strong>Industrial process airflow</strong>: Combustion air, exhaust ventilation, and drying/curing oven airflow checks.</li>
</ul>

